<div class="card readme-card"><h2>Complete app guide</h2><p>All sponsor framework explanations and app operating instructions are available here. Older source wording is reconciled to the current approved workflow; company policy and live checks remain explicitly labeled.</p><div class="registry-guide-search"><label for="registryGuideSearch">Search the complete guide</label><input id="registryGuideSearch" type="search" placeholder="Category, evidence, approvals, expiry, scaling…" oninput="filterRegistryGuide_()"><p id="registryGuideSearchStatus" role="status" aria-live="polite">Browse the complete sponsor reference and app guide below. Search opens matching sections.</p></div><section id="sponsorGuide-root" class="sponsor-guide" aria-labelledby="sponsorGuide-title">
<h3 id="sponsorGuide-title">Sponsor framework and complete operating guide</h3>
<p>Open a topic below for the full explanations from the supplied Action Registry worksheets and sponsor notes, reconciled to the current approved workflow. Current policy inputs and live checks are labeled explicitly.</p>
<details id="sponsorGuide-purpose" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="purpose"><summary>1. Purpose, operating principles and the complete workflow</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>The Action Registry brings proposals, review, execution proof, impact assessment and verified follow-through into one internal operational record. It serves as an action-oriented diagnostic health gauge for account stability and supports accountability across sites and departments through the CARL Strategic Governance Framework: <strong>Consistent, Accountable, Resilient, Locked-In</strong>.</p><p>Track <strong>what will be done, who owns it, what success means, when it is due and which evidence supports the result</strong>. Keep proposal approval, execution status, KPI outcome, effectiveness and follow-up closure separate. A task can be Completed while its effect is still Insufficient Evidence.</p><p><strong>Workflow:</strong> Register → Review / return / decline → Approved &amp; Ongoing → Execute / update / proof → Completed lock → Assess impact → Follow-up / independent verification → Reporting and eligible shared Chat notices.</p><p>Operations create the actual actions. The registry does not generate them or predict causality. Specific, measurable proposals can be reviewed and improved; weak ideas are not silently deleted to make the portfolio appear successful. Internal action tracking and client-facing reporting are separate; any external sharing needs its own approved audience.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Workspace</th><th scope="col">Use it to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Executive Dashboard</td><td>Filter the recorded portfolio; inspect exceptions, review accountability, effectiveness and stability.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Submission &amp; Updates</td><td>Register proposals; open details; update eligible execution, upload proof, inspect history or revise.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Approvals &amp; Feedback</td><td>Review current proposals, give return/decline reasons and add independent leadership comments.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Effectiveness &amp; Decisions</td><td>Assess impact; record next decisions; track and verify commitments; review scaling authorization.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Gantt</td><td>Read the planned date timeline for the same selected scope.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Readme</td><td>Find definitions, field guidance, permissions, operating steps and limitations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>My Work and Admin are later approved additions. My Work collects current authorized next actions. Admin manages user profiles and access; it does not automatically grant access to every business record.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-framework" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="framework"><summary>2. Five categories, thirteen types and all photographed activity examples</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p><strong>Choose Category → Type → a specific Activity / Action Steps → measurement target → effectiveness review → an eligible scoped approver.</strong> The original worksheet called the approver L1; the current approved rule uses the eligible shared approval pool, with no mandatory named L1. The worksheet labels the category layer Strategic Intent / Stability Signal. Category expresses strategic intent; Type expresses the tactical approach; Activity explains granular execution.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Category / strategic intent</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Intervention</td><td>Corrective actions to fix performance variance, KPI drops or execution gaps within existing operational bounds.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Escalation</td><td>Upward or cross-functional action when frontline operations cannot resolve a blocker, such as a product bug, ambiguous policy or capacity constraint.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Standardization</td><td>Embed a verified fix or baseline standard into everyday routines, documentation and workflows.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Scaling</td><td>Replicate a validated local best practice across teams, LOBs or sites under the appropriate governance.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Engagement</td><td>Increase morale, participation and team drive. Engagement complements other actions; it is not the main driver of KPI improvement.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Definition</th><th scope="col">Worksheet activity examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Intervention</td><td>Behavior Realignment</td><td>Direct, tactical engagements focused on correcting individual or squad-level execution variance.</td><td>1:1 Targeted Coaching; QA Side-by-Side Shadowing; Outlier Performance Plan; Disciplinary Action / Removals</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Intervention</td><td>Capability Building</td><td>Rapid-response learning interventions when knowledge or skill gaps are identified as root drivers.</td><td>Refresher Training Blitz; Knowledge Packet Delivery; Nested Incubation Support; Huddle Upskilling</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Intervention</td><td>Tactical Capacity &amp; Operational Control</td><td>Short-term workforce or queue adjustments to protect service levels and prevent operational burnout.</td><td>Concurrency Override; Mandatory Overtime / Shrinkage Clawback; Temporary Channel Floor Shifting; Queue Threshold Triggering</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Escalation</td><td>Product &amp; Bug Escalation (for long-standing issues only)</td><td>Formal friction logs and tickets to client product or engineering teams when systems or software break.</td><td>Product Incident Ticket; Merchant Journey Friction Report; System Outage Alerting</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Escalation</td><td>Policy &amp; Knowledge Clarification</td><td>Requests to resolve ambiguous policies, broken macros or conflicting guidelines.</td><td>Escalation for Policy Clarification; Macro/Help Center Article Gap Request; Edge Case Guidance Request</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Escalation</td><td>Resource &amp; Cross-Functional Request</td><td>Formal appeals for executive decisions, additional budget, headcount or cross-department intervention.</td><td>Leadership Attention Request; Capacity Expansion Proposal; Cross-Functional SLA Escalation</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Standardization</td><td>SOP &amp; Workflow Institutionalization</td><td>Update core operating procedures and training materials so improvements survive beyond the initial focus period.</td><td>Knowledge Base Article / Macro Update; SOP / Workflow Redesign; QA Rubric / Calibration Standard Adjustment</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Standardization</td><td>Baseline Governance &amp; Routine Lock-in</td><td>Establish permanent operational checkpoints and audit mechanisms to sustain execution discipline.</td><td>Audit Routine Creation; Team Leader Execution Standard Update; WFM Baseline Requirement Lock</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Standardization</td><td>Governance Projects</td><td>Launch an initiative, such as streamlining WBR or a new dashboard, that improves overall account governance even when not directly tied to a numeric performance KPI.</td><td>BIDS, QA, LX, SLT, or Global Ops initiatives that may not be tied directly to performance but improve overall account governance</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Scaling</td><td>Replication &amp; Pilot Rollout</td><td>Expand a locally validated best practice to additional LOBs, squads or sites under controlled monitoring.</td><td>Multi-Site Pilot Launch; LOB Cross-Replication; Best-Practice Playbook Distribution</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Scaling</td><td>Global Institutionalization</td><td>Formally adopt a proven local innovation as the global standard across the account network.</td><td>Global Standard Operating Model Update; Account-Wide Training Curriculum Release; Global Tooling / Automation Rollout</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Engagement</td><td>Incentivized Activity</td><td>Monetary or non-monetary activity supporting morale, participation and team drive; complementary to the main KPI actions.</td><td>Town Halls; Blitz Incentive; Team Building</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Engagement</td><td>Non-Incentivized Activity</td><td>The worksheet repeats the monetary/non-monetary morale, participation and team-drive definition; complementary to the main KPI actions.</td><td>The photographed example cell was blank. Later configuration notes permit genuine non-incentivized Town Halls / Team Building; Blitz Incentive does not belong here.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Action_Framework supplies the active types, definitions, examples and order. A type must belong to its selected category. Examples guide the author; they are not automatic actions or an exhaustive allowlist. Preserve useful site-specific activity wording and explain its measurable result. The long-standing-issue qualifier applies to Product &amp; Bug Escalation.</p><p>The worksheet uses the same broad morale/participation description for both Engagement types. The visible Non-Incentivized example cell was blank; later legitimate configured examples are distinguished above from the original photograph. Engagement remains complementary, not a substitute for corrective or institutional improvements.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-stability" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="stability"><summary>3. Four stability interpretations, score and provisional numerical rules</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">Sponsor interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Reactive</td><td>High Intervention volume signals instability, execution variance and firefighting.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Emerging</td><td>High Escalation volume signals systemic blockers, product/policy gaps or cross-functional dependencies.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Maturing</td><td>A balanced mix transitioning toward Standardization and Scaling. A leading mature mix can still lack the evidence required for Locked-In.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">CARL (Locked-In)</td><td>Strong Standardization and Scaling show institutionalized, self-sustaining improvement, subject to the configured evidence requirements.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Maturity score = 100 × (Standardization + Scaling) ÷ eligible core plans.</strong> This describes the action mix, not actual KPI health, financial benefit or causation. Engagement is shown separately. Category-mix reporting can include a different population from the stability denominator; read the labels.</p><p>The stability population uses authoritative current-version approval, eligible Ongoing / On Hold plans and recently Completed plans within the configured window. Pending/returned proposals, declined/cancelled plans and legacy plans without recorded approval are excluded. Older active commitments remain visible; creation date alone does not remove them.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Proposed DRAFT-1 setting</th><th scope="col">Value</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Minimum_Core_Plans</td><td>5</td><td>Minimum eligible core sample before classifying.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Dominance_Share</td><td>50%</td><td>Unambiguous Intervention/Escalation dominance.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">LockedIn_Mature_Share</td><td>70%</td><td>Standardization + Scaling share for a Locked-In candidate.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Minimum_Validated_Share</td><td>60%</td><td>Recent evidence-backed Validated coverage among core plans.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Minimum_Sustained_Validated_Share</td><td>50%</td><td>Recent Validated and Sustained coverage.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Evidence_Max_Age_Days</td><td>90 days</td><td>Maximum assessment observation age for qualifying evidence.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Completed_Lookback_Days</td><td>90 days</td><td>Recent Completed inclusion window.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>These numbers are proposals, not sponsor-approved standards.</strong> Review Rules_Status, Rules_Version, evaluation date, denominator, exclusions, evidence coverage, reason and suggested leadership action. A maintainer changes controlled rules only after business review. The 90-day windows are classification windows, not a deletion or retention policy.</p><p>Ties can produce Mixed / Review Needed; small samples can produce Insufficient Data. Missing/duplicate IDs, unsupported eligible categories, bad versions or unmatched approval/assessment history withhold classification for Data Review Required. Unknown data is not a healthy zero. Category or execution-status filters show Filtered Mix Only because they select the classification inputs.</p><p>All filters apply to the displayed counts. An authorized unfiltered enterprise view combines Global-tagged and site/department plans; selecting Global alone selects only Global-tagged plans. No historical state or score is invented for dates before recorded history.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-fields" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="fields"><summary>4. Registration fields and every photographed dropdown option</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>Use <strong>Register New Action Plan</strong> to start a blank proposal. Give the plan a meaningful name and specific Action Steps, not a vague action description. Identify the actual owner, site or literal site/department scope, separate Department, Channel, LOB, Cohort and applicable cohort details, category/type, planned start/end dates, objective and glide-path availability. A new plan receives a generated ID and begins Pending Review / Not Started.</p><p>A targeted cohort needs its details in the dedicated field. Describe the permitted aggregate group without identifying prohibited individuals. These dropdown lists are independent: horizontal alignment in the photographed worksheet does not mean a KPI is tied to the Channel, LOB or Cohort on the same row.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Documented options / guidance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Site / Department scope</td><td>Global, ARN, HTW, MTQ and any separately controlled configured scopes. MYQ is the legacy alias displayed under MTQ; keep historical IDs unchanged. Global is literal.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Department</td><td>Quality; Training; WFM. This is a separate descriptive dimension, not automatic cross-site authority.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Channel</td><td>Chat; Phones; ASYNC; Overall.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">LOB</td><td>MS Core; MS Core - Premium; Tier 2 - Money/Live Assist/TMS; Plus; Retail; App Review; Biz Ops; Buyer Support; All LOBs.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Cohort</td><td>Targeted (Specify in the comments); 0-3 Months; 3-6 Months; 6-9 Months; 9-12 Months; 12 Months +; Peak (PACE); Achieving (PACE); Concerning (PACE); Escalated (PACE); Overall - Teammates; Leadership - Team Leaders; Leadership - Operations Managers; Leadership - SLT.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">KPI</td><td>Smiley; AFTR; Quality; AHT; Productivity (TPH/SPD); Attrition (Retention); Shrinkage (OOO); Interval Compliance; Schedule Adherence; Compliance (CCA/ZTP); Compliance (FRM); Hygiene.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Hygiene examples</td><td>The worksheet expands Hygiene as Aux Toggling, Credits, Cherry Picking, etc. Approved unit, direction and operational definition still come from Settings.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Dates and ownership</td><td>Use the actual accountable active work identity and planned dates. Saved submission/update times are system records; they are not proof of actual execution or observation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Settings supplies current approved choices and KPI units/directions. Do not guess whether Compliance or Hygiene is a rate, count or another unit. Existing legitimate configured values and historical labels are preserved. A title such as Director or Senior Manager does not grant submission or review permission by itself.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-measurement" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="measurement"><summary>5. Baseline, current, goal, arithmetic, units and glide path</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p><strong>Numeric KPI</strong> uses the agreed baseline, latest current result, target, unit and improvement direction. Higher is better and Lower is better are configured per KPI. The same numerical sign can mean different business outcomes, so do not infer improvement merely from a positive difference.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Metric</th><th scope="col">Definition / interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Goal Progress %</td><td>(Current − Baseline) ÷ (Goal − Baseline) × 100. Not applicable when Goal = Baseline. Internally the ratio 0.5 represents 50%; negative progress and values above 100% remain meaningful displayed results.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Performance Change</td><td>Current − Baseline. A signed change, despite the original template wording absolute difference.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Gap to Goal</td><td>Goal − Current, signed for both higher- and lower-is-better KPIs. Direction controls interpretation, not the arithmetic sign.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Days Remaining</td><td>Target End Date − current Manila calendar date. Negative means the deadline has passed. Missing/invalid dates are not due-today zeroes.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Goal Attainment</td><td>Current has reached/passed Goal in the configured improvement direction. Missing measurements/direction show Insufficient Data.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Milestone Completion Rate</td><td>Completed actions ÷ actions assigned to a defined milestone × 100, only when an actual milestone grouping exists. The app does not invent milestone groups or extract them from glide links.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Execution / schedule health</td><td>Not Started, Ongoing, On Hold, Completed and Cancelled are execution states. On Track / At Risk / Overdue describe schedule exposure under the controlled calculation; they are not impact ratings.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Percentages use the configured percent unit; deltas are percentage points. AHT accepts HH:MM:SS or decimal minutes and uses canonical minutes; counts use non-negative whole numbers when configured as counts. Missing or invalid measurements are not converted to a healthy zero.</p><p>Goal Met, Improving, Unchanged, Worsening and Insufficient Data describe the numeric comparison. They do not prove the action caused the movement. The visual progress bar may be bounded for display while the percentage label keeps the actual calculation.</p><p><strong>Governance outcome</strong> is available for Standardization / Governance Projects. Record Current problem, Expected outcome, Acceptance criteria and Verification method. Numeric KPI/baseline/goal stay blank. Owners explain qualitative progress; assessors describe evidence against each criterion. Governance Projects can use Numeric KPI instead when appropriate. A revision can change the method, but it does not inherit an unrelated numeric result or prior current-version assessment.</p><p><strong>Time-phased Glide Path:</strong> choose Yes or No. Yes requires an HTTPS Google Drive, Docs, Sheets or Slides source. No means no stored glide link. The app retains the link; it does not read milestones, ingest KPI series, evaluate actual-versus-glide trajectories or change source sharing. A valid URL alone does not prove content quality or permission.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-approval" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="approval"><summary>6. Review, leadership feedback, revision and approval independence</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>A proposal is reviewed by an active <strong>Can_Approve</strong> account with explicit read access and matching Approval_Sites scope. Any eligible independent member of the shared pool may decide the current version; there is no mandatory named L1. Review accountability shows eligible reviewers and waiting age, not an assignment of one sole reviewer or a performance score.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Worksheet / app decision</th><th scope="col">What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Accept / Approve</td><td>The plan is SMART—specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound—and ready for execution. Save the current-version review; execution becomes Ongoing. Approval is permission to execute, not proof that it worked.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Revise / Return for Revision</td><td>The proposal needs refinement. Record a meaningful reason; the owner revises and resubmits. Material revision creates a new version, resets to Not Started / Pending Review and requires approval again.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Decline</td><td>The proposal must not be implemented. Record a meaningful reason; the declined proposal becomes Cancelled.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Leadership feedback</td><td>Can_Comment adds a separate dated observation about feasibility, vague steps or other concerns. It does not change performance, execution, proposal approval or effectiveness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Regular approvers cannot decide a plan they own or submitted. The server uses current ownership and matching submission provenance, not a browser claim. Missing or inconsistent legacy provenance is a reconciliation issue; the app does not guess historical submitters.</p><p>An <strong>admin self-approval exception</strong> is an explicit separate choice. The account still needs Active, Can_Admin, Can_Approve, current read and approval scope, trustworthy provenance and current versions, plus a meaningful exception reason. The exception and actor/version/conflict are logged. Can_Admin alone does not approve a plan or bypass scope, completed locks or stale versions.</p><p>Reopening a stale screen does not reserve a decision. If another reviewer or owner changed the record, reload before deciding. One valid decision resolves a pending version; reuse of an unchanged accepted request returns its saved result rather than creating a duplicate.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-execution" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="execution"><summary>7. Owner updates, evidence and the Completed lock</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>The active current owner updates approved, editable execution with current performance, status and an explanatory progress comment. Normal execution updates keep the approved plan version; material proposal changes use Revise Plan and reapproval. Historical unenrolled plans retain the compatible legacy path without invented historical approvals.</p><p>Evidence may be added after registration. <strong>Current-version proof is required before a new transition to Completed.</strong> The owner uploads a description and a non-empty permitted file of at most 10 MB: PDF, PNG, JPEG, TXT, CSV, DOCX, XLSX or PPTX, with matching extension/MIME metadata. Files are stored in the configured restricted company Drive location; the app does not grant sharing.</p><p>Proof must belong to the same action and plan version and remain available in the configured folder. A moved, trashed, foreign or unavailable file cannot satisfy the new completion gate. File presence proves neither evidence quality nor causal impact; permitted links also require human content and access review.</p><p><strong>Completed execution is read-only.</strong> It cannot be reopened, revised, receive a new execution result/comment or receive another execution-proof upload through the app, including by the owner or admin. Existing details, results, evidence and history stay readable. Authorized effectiveness assessments, separate leadership comments and independently verified follow-ups remain possible because they append their own records.</p><p>The photographed worksheet originally required an attachment at initial submission. The later confirmed rule supersedes that timing: register first if needed, then supply proof before completion. Use sanitized artifacts and review filenames, descriptions, screenshots and metadata before uploading.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-effectiveness" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="effectiveness"><summary>8. Effectiveness ratings, observation windows, weekly review and rework</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>An eligible active <strong>Can_Audit</strong> assessor evaluates Ongoing, On Hold or Completed plans with current-version approval where required. Record the actual observation start/end, expected impact, behavior changes, observed impact, other contributing factors, expectations, sustained result, action relationship, rationale, evidence and next accountable decision. Explain confounding factors instead of assuming correlation is causation.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Rating</th><th scope="col">Meaning and required interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Validated</td><td>Clear evidence of the desired impact; expectations Met and a Supported relationship to the action. Evidence remains required.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Partially Effective</td><td>Some movement or impact, but results are insufficient or inconsistent. Explain what changed and what still needs work.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">No Observable Effect</td><td>No demonstrated effect under the required conditions: Completed execution, expectations Not Met and supporting evidence. Explain execution/root-cause problems.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Insufficient Evidence</td><td>Too early or insufficient proof to reach an impact conclusion. This is legitimate even for a Completed action.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Not Yet Assessed</td><td>No recorded current-version assessment. It is not the same as a completed assessment of Insufficient Evidence. Unknown/unavailable history must be shown as a data issue, not invented assessment status.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Suggested owner, objective and dates are starting points for confirmation. A first observation start can reuse the planned start; subsequent suggestions can follow the prior current-version observation end. Dates remain editable to match evidence, including overlapping periods; future observations are blocked. A saved KPI snapshot records the latest values at assessment time, not a period-specific aggregate.</p><p><strong>Every week, operations reviews every eligible action.</strong> Use the weekly evaluation coverage/attention queue and the saved next-review commitments. A weekly entry is supported by the recorded current-version assessment and its audit time. A late review does not automatically become a negative rating, a Stop decision or a withdrawn commitment. Initial deadlines, recurring exceptions and continuing review of completed plans still need their approved policy.</p><p>The sponsor asks unmeasurable, No Observable Effect or Insufficient Evidence actions to be revisited. Record the evidence gap, root cause and corrective follow-up; use Adjust and an eligible owner revision/reapproval when the proposal must change. Preserve a Completed record and use an appropriate follow-on action where necessary. Do not fabricate positive evidence, delete an unsuccessful action or silently rewrite its history.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-scaling" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="scaling"><summary>9. Next decisions and the separate governing scaling decision</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Decision</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Continue</td><td>Keep the action under observation and record the next evidence/review commitment.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Adjust</td><td>Change the approach and record corrective follow-through. This decision alone does not revise the approved proposal.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Stop</td><td>Record the decision and required follow-through. It does not itself cancel or rewrite execution status.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Recommend Scaling</td><td>Propose wider adoption only with the documented positive evidence and risk conditions. It is not authorization to roll out.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Scalability test</th><th scope="col">Sponsor explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Impact</td><td>Proven measurable improvement in the intended KPI or governance outcome.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Repeatability</td><td>Results can be repeated without depending on one exceptional leader or temporary hero effort.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Transferability</td><td>The practice can work across the intended sites, LOBs, channels, tenures or environments, with adaptations explained.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Sustainability</td><td>The improvement survives beyond the initial intervention/focus period.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Low risk</td><td>Operational, compliance, security and merchant-experience risks are acceptable and explained.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Recommend Scaling requires <strong>Validated + Sustained</strong>, supporting evidence, repeatability and transferability explanations, and an <strong>Acceptable</strong> risk review. The source plan/assessment and the destination conditions must remain traceable. A favorable rating or maturity score does not authorize expansion.</p><p>Use the separate Scaling authorization panel to inspect the current recommendation and record Approved or Declined only when governance has configured the named independent decision-maker. That account still needs current Can_Approve and existing source <em>and every target</em> read/approval scope. The source owner/submitter cannot use this conservative governing path, and admin status supplies no bypass. If governance configuration is absent, the controls remain disabled.</p><p>The governing record stores source versions/assessment, explicit target scopes, conditions, reason and server-authored actor/date. History is append-only and shown only where all its target scopes are readable. Stale or uncertain outcomes require reload/reconciliation. Recording a decision does not create target plans, grant access, send a rollout or perform deployment. Actual governing authority and any additional rollout conditions require the business owner’s confirmation.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-followups" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="followups"><summary>10. Commitments, independent verification, deadlines and extensions</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>Each saved assessment creates a delivery commitment and a next effectiveness review commitment. Continue with the same completion/review date can use one combined review commitment. A commitment has a concrete deliverable, active accountable owner, independent assigned Can_Audit verifier, source assessment and dates. Earlier commitments stay open until explicitly resolved.</p><p>Owners record progress or blockage, submit evidence, or request an extension with a reason. The assigned independent verifier checks proof, closes or returns the commitment, explicitly withdraws with a reason, or handles the allowed extension/reopen workflow. The owner cannot independently verify their own work. Closure proves that commitment was delivered; it does not automatically validate impact or authorize scaling.</p><p>Extensions preserve the original due date and append the request/decision history. Progress notes do not reset overdue status. Manila calendar deadlines include weekends; a commitment becomes overdue after its deadline day ends. Verification has a separate deadline and escalation phase, normally two calendar days after evidence submission under the configured rule.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Queue / action</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Needs attention</td><td>Prioritize items needing delivery, review or resolution.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Assigned to me / Mine</td><td>Current accountable owner/verifier work within your scope.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Open / All open</td><td>Unresolved commitments.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Overdue</td><td>The applicable local deadline has passed.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Escalated</td><td>Confirmed overdue reminders have accumulated strikes.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Awaiting verification</td><td>Owner proof is submitted; responsibility moves to the assigned verifier.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Verified closed / Closed this week</td><td>Independent verification is recorded, with history retained.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">All commitments</td><td>Visible open and historical commitments under the current permissions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-reporting" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="reporting"><summary>11. Dashboard, denominators, review accountability, Gantt and capture</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>Filters and search describe the selected authorized portfolio: site/scope, KPI, execution status, owner, category and Department. The same selected data drives action rows, site snapshots, approval/impact views, category mix, narrative and Gantt. A Department filter narrows visible records; it does not grant cross-site access. Missing departments have a Not recorded choice where provided.</p><p>Active counts exclude Completed and Cancelled. Schedule cards show On Track, At Risk and Overdue under the controlled calculation. Goal Met respects KPI direction. Top Exceptions prioritizes Overdue and At Risk; the report states when only a bounded subset is shown. Open a row for full steps and history.</p><p>Approval accountability shows current-version states, eligible reviewers and waiting age by site/department. Effectiveness counts use recorded current-version ratings, with Not Yet Assessed and unknown/data issues distinct. Category mix is descriptive and can include Cancelled plans; the stability core denominator has different explicit eligibility rules. Do not substitute a completion rate, goal-attainment count or maturity score for validated impact.</p><p>The executive narrative summarizes saved data and current scope using deterministic text and literal excerpts. It is not AI/Gemini analysis and does not claim causality. The current review-week view is Sunday–Saturday recorded activity in Manila; it is not an inferred newly-at-risk/recovered transition or a comparison to the actual last leadership meeting. My History comparisons require real saved snapshots under the same access/filter context.</p><p>Gantt shows planned start/end dates within the selected scope, with status labels and table information as an alternative to color. Impossible, missing or reversed dates are data issues, not dates to silently normalize.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Capture view</th><th scope="col">Use / limits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Executive</td><td>Presentation view with selected scope, concise totals, a stated bounded top-exception list, narrative and qualified stability.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Full Report</td><td>All authorized selected rows with pagination, repeated headers, page/row ranges and print layout.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Snapshot metadata</td><td>Check server snapshot time, successful load time, capture time, timezone, filters, release and rule identity. Failed/stale loads disable current capture.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Privacy and controls</td><td>Owner column/filter identity is hidden as requested, but free text, filenames, excerpts and other fields can still identify people. Inspect the entire output before sharing. Esc / Exit leaves capture; printing omits the exit control.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-chat" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="chat"><summary>12. Shared Chat notices, schedules, strikes and safe recovery</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>The app posts bounded callouts to the <strong>approved shared Google Chat space</strong> through a private incoming webhook. It sends no Gmail/email or individual Chat direct messages. Staff names/emails identify accountability in the card; they are not delivery addresses. Cards can show action/ID, status, owner, deadline, next step, escalation detail and the configured Open Action Registry link. Approvals and form submissions occur in the app, not inside the webhook card.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Notice / control</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Plan Updates</td><td>Newly saved registration, revision, review, execution update, feedback, assessment and follow-up changes. No replay of all historical records.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Assignment Notices</td><td>Announce open commitments and accountable staff; assignment notices add no overdue strikes.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Due Reminders</td><td>Eligible commitments due soon or overdue, using the current owner/verifier phase.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Preview</td><td>Inspect the permitted proposed content without sending. Available to appropriately scoped audit/admin users.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Pause / Resume / Manual send</td><td>Current Can_Admin plus permitted business scope controls delivery. Audience approval and verified webhook/readiness are separate.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Schedules</td><td>When explicitly installed and authorized: queued changes/assignments approximately every 15 minutes; daily reminders around 09:00–10:00 Manila. A stored trigger-owner name is not proof of a successful scheduled run.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Chat content, previews, sends and maintenance are limited by current approved scope. Scheduled runs recheck the recorded owner’s active account, capability and grants; they do not turn an admin or literal Global tag into all-record access. Confirm the shared-space audience, including guests, before activation. Webhook credentials stay in server Script Properties, never forms, filenames, screenshots or source examples.</p><p>Confirmed commitment notices suppress repeats for the same owner/verifier phase for 24 hours. Confirmed overdue reminders accumulate at most three strikes: owner attention, mapped manager attention, then designated executive attention in the same space. Missing routing is a configuration exception. Strikes do not automatically Stop an action. While proof awaits verification, the verifier has separate timing and escalation.</p><p>A notification attempt is logged SENDING before transport; confirmed HTTP acceptance becomes SENT. Acceptance is not proof anyone read it. Rejected attempts remain FAILED; uncertain transport/server acceptance stays SENDING until a maintainer confirms Sent or Not Sent. Do not blindly retry an unknown outcome. A Chat problem does not undo a saved business change: do not register the action again just to resend its notice.</p><p>After three rejected plan-notice attempts, a maintainer corrects the cause, records a reason and authorizes an explicit retry. Accepted notices cannot be resent through that retry control. Clear Failed Notices acknowledges reviewed failures and hides them from the active exception view while preserving original rows, retry limits and history. It neither resends nor fixes the underlying cause.</p><p>Processing remains bounded: plan-change deliveries send at most ten notices per run; eligible commitment runs are also bounded. Later items remain for later runs. Preview real contents before resuming delivery, and keep integrations paused during setup or unresolved incidents.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-access" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="access"><summary>13. Permissions, scope, user administration, expiry and handover</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Authority</th><th scope="col">What it permits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Verified active account</td><td>An exact authorized TaskUs work identity with one active Access_Control entry. Actual boolean grants are required; the string TRUE and a role label are not permissions.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Can_Submit</td><td>Register within the approved business scope. Job titles and historical named provisioning exceptions do not create hardcoded bypasses.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Owner</td><td>Update, upload and revise eligible owned plans; no bypass of current approval, versions or Completed locks.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Can_Approve + Approval_Sites</td><td>Review within explicit scopes, with current read access and independence rules.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Can_Audit</td><td>Assess eligible actions; follow-up verification also requires the assigned independent verifier.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Can_Comment</td><td>Add separate dated leadership feedback.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Can_Admin</td><td>Manage user profiles globally and perform authorized maintenance/Chat operations. Business reads and other workflow capabilities remain separately scoped.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Blank business grants deny protected reads. Global is a literal scope, not a wildcard. A separate Department value does not grant access to that department in every location. Access to the web app, backing Sheet, evidence folder, project editor and Chat space are separate permissions. A domain-only deployment or a shared Drive link is not sufficient proof of correct access.</p><p>Admins manage names, role labels, department/title, active status, capabilities, scopes, account expiry and temporary permissions. Work email is the identity key: enroll the correct identity and hand over work instead of rewriting historical authorship. Removing access means deactivating/revoking the profile while retaining required history. Preview and reason/version checks protect changes. Another admin must change your own security grants; retain a permanent nonexpiring recovery admin.</p><p>Account expiry blocks the account’s app access. Temporary grants end their specified capability/scope at the configured expiry while valid permanent grants remain. Expiry entry/display is UTC; operational due dates remain Manila. The server checks current access, rather than granting rights from browser time or an old screen.</p><p>Owner handover requires a scoped admin and an eligible authorized target. It appends a new proposal/submission version and returns the plan for approval, preserving previous owner/history/evidence. Completed/Cancelled execution is locked. Existing follow-up owners/verifiers are not silently reassigned.</p><p>For joiners, movers and leavers, review app grants and separately review Workspace account, deployment, Sheet/Drive/Chat access, project editors and trigger ownership. Corporate MFA/SSO and periodic access reviews are tenant/company controls; the app does not add a separate password/MFA system. The older BRD proposes quarterly reviews and joiner/mover/leaver reviews; the approved company schedule must still be confirmed.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-privacy" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="privacy"><summary>14. Permitted data, storage, sharing and security limits</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>Use operational descriptions, aggregate KPI measures and sanitized governance evidence. Only necessary staff work identity belongs in the designated ownership, authorization, review, verification and audit fields. The registry does not require customer, merchant or business-owner personal records. Small cohorts, named outliers or a combination of fields can still identify people.</p><p>Do not enter customer/merchant names, personal emails, phone numbers, addresses, payment/card data, PHI, account credentials, tokens, raw conversations or ticket/order/merchant identifiers that enable account lookup. Check plan names, Action Steps, comments, cohort details, descriptions, source links, filenames, metadata, Chat excerpts and screenshots. A permitted file extension or Google URL does not certify its content or sharing.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Data / system</th><th scope="col">What is stored and where</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">Staff / program records</td><td>Work identities and grants; scope, steps, dates, numeric/governance objectives, statuses and assessments in controlled Sheets and authorized app views.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Evidence</td><td>Contents in the configured company Drive folder; filename, description, ID, uploader, time and link in evidence logs.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Histories and notifications</td><td>Version snapshots, actors, timestamps, follow-up events, queued excerpts and delivery outcomes in supporting logs; selected approved summaries in shared Chat.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Server configuration</td><td>Workbook/evidence IDs, approved policies and webhook credentials in Script Properties; access restricted through project-editor governance.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Browser and extra copies</td><td>User browser receives authorized data; reports/screenshots create additional copies. This version uses system fonts and does not request Google Fonts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Data flow:</strong> authorized staff browser → Apps Script → registry Sheets; accepted proof → restricted Drive + metadata log; eligible saved-event summaries → outbox → approved shared Chat webhook. Linked Docs/Sheets/Slides/Drive remain separately governed. No Shopify operational API, customer-system ingestion or Gemini service is added.</p><p>Server identity, object/scope/capability/version checks, bounded validation, literal spreadsheet text, safe display/card text, locks, durable request records, restricted URLs and upload limits reduce application risks. Public failures use bounded reference messages. The app lock does not constrain a person who directly edits a backing Sheet or script. Restrict editors; sheet logs/digests are not an immutable external audit trail.</p><p>There is no automatic PII detector, redaction, DLP certification or malware-content scan. Human review and organizational controls remain necessary. HTTPS/Google hosting and provider encryption claims do not establish the actual tenant’s policy compliance, key settings, network restrictions or approved audience. Company IT/InfoSec/Privacy must verify those settings.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-operations" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="operations"><summary>15. Configuration, installation, recovery, retention and acceptance</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>Keep the four current files together: <strong>Code.gs, Index.html, appsscript.json and Installer.gs</strong>. Read the Installer opening instructions for the exact existing-project replacement/removal and property steps; do not rerun historical release commands from old notes. Back up the source/deployment versions, entire workbook/history/formulas and private properties, and stop other writers before approved migration. Saving source alone does not update an existing versioned web deployment.</p><p>The installer’s controlled sequence is PREVIEW → APPLY_SCHEMA → PREVIEW_RETRY → APPLY_RETRY → STATUS. Review the exact preview and set its approval hash before apply. Retry migration processes bounded batches, keeps original keys and verifies copied results before removing accepted old properties. Held or uncertain requests stay blocked for governed review. Never delete their evidence simply to enable another save.</p><p>For an approved empty workbook only, the optional sponsor math preset installs the four explicit arithmetic definitions into empty fresh calculation columns; it requires Manila timezone and binds exact formulas to the preview. It does not invent Schedule Health thresholds or KPI unit/direction values, replace live formulas or grant ordinary business access. A fresh bootstrap enrolls only the maintenance admin with blank business scope; approved capabilities/scopes still need provisioning.</p><p>Settings controls choices and metric configuration; Action_Framework controls active types, definitions and examples; Access_Control controls identities/capabilities/scopes; Stability_Rules controls the visible provisional method/version. Setup is additive and preserves historical IDs, permissions, unknown columns and existing definitions/formulas. Formatting-equivalent framework rows are reconciled without deleting inactive alternatives or approving changed meaning. A successful setup/configuration check is not sponsor sign-off or a proof of formula results.</p><p><strong>Retention and legal holds:</strong> the owner/IT/InfoSec must approve retention and disposal for master records, all histories, evidence, outbox/notification/security logs, Chat copies and backups. The older security template asks for at least 180 days of application/audit logs, but actual enforcement/provider policy and business-record retention remain to be confirmed. Classification lookback windows do not authorize deletion. Do not purge accepted retry keys or historical logs in a way that could permit duplicate saves.</p><p><strong>Incident response and recovery:</strong> pause affected operations/Chat, notify the designated company incident contact/maintainer, preserve evidence and use an approved sanitized continuity log. Restore only from approved source/workbook/evidence/configuration backups, reconcile IDs, accepted requests and uncertain notices, verify access and data, then resume through the approved process. Company owners must specify backup frequency, backup retention, recovery-point/time objectives (acceptable data loss and restoration time), authorized recipients and a proven restore test; version history alone is not a backup plan.</p><p>Weekly executive business review is a management process. Monthly/quarterly access, framework, pilot-effectiveness and retention reviews are governance cadences to approve, not automatically installed email jobs.</p><p>Before live use, confirm the actual workbook/project/deployment identity and audience, real user roles and denied access, narrow Drive grants and ACLs, formula outputs, evidence upload/cleanup, concurrency/retries/recovery, shared Chat audience and trigger execution. Use synthetic examples for training and UAT. Record separate business, IT/InfoSec/AppSec and production authorization outcomes, with the responsible owner/date. A local test result, flag, stored trigger owner or an old delivery screenshot is not live acceptance.</p><p>Current policy inputs still needing confirmation include cross-site department authority if desired, detailed recurring-review exceptions/closure, actual governing scaling accounts/target conditions, accepted stability numbers, controlled KPI/Schedule Health configuration, Chat audience/operational authority, retention/privacy/incident ownership and live platform checks. The app keeps implemented capabilities and missing configuration distinct; it does not invent approvals.</p><div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Maintainer property names — values belong in Script Properties</th><th scope="col">Purpose / safety boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="readme-term">REGISTRY_SPREADSHEET_ID; REGISTRY_READ_POLICY</td><td>Exact approved workbook; retain APPROVAL_SITES. A workbook ID is not a permission grant.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">REGISTRY_MAINTENANCE_OWNER; REGISTRY_MAINTENANCE_TARGET_CONFIRMED</td><td>Named approved work-account maintainer and the same exact target workbook.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">REGISTRY_MAINTENANCE_BACKUP_REFERENCE; REGISTRY_MAINTENANCE_CHANGE_ID</td><td>Recoverable backup/change references and the corresponding confirmed window/backup flags listed in Installer.gs.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">REGISTRY_MAINTENANCE_APPROVED_PREVIEW_HASH; REGISTRY_OPERATION_MIGRATION_APPROVAL</td><td>Exact reviewed schema and retry previews. Rerun preview after state changes or each retry batch.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">REGISTRY_OPERATION_MAINTENANCE; REGISTRY_OPERATION_LEDGER_READY; REGISTRY_OPERATION_LEGACY_WRITERS_RETIRED</td><td>Pause/cutover gates. Set retirement only after actual other writers stop; the flag does not stop old code.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">REGISTRY_OPERATION_MAINTAINER_EMAIL</td><td>Explicit authorized operation-migration/recovery account.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">EVIDENCE_FOLDER_ID; REGISTRY_EVIDENCE_READY</td><td>Approved evidence destination and separately verified resource-readiness gate.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">REGISTRY_CHAT_WEBHOOK_URL; REGISTRY_CHAT_MODE; REGISTRY_CHAT_AUDIENCE_APPROVED</td><td>Private destination credential; PAUSED during setup; approved audience and verified activation before LIVE delivery. Never display the secret value.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">FOLLOWUP_APP_URL</td><td>Optional existing approved /exec URL for the Open Action Registry card link.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">REGISTRY_INSTALL_SPONSOR_MATH; REGISTRY_APPROVED_ARRAY_FORMULAS_JSON</td><td>FRESH selects the four sponsor formulas by default only when the preset property is absent and no custom formulas are set. Set FALSE or an explicit blank to leave the preset unselected, or supply separately reviewed complete custom formulas. Never select both; see Installer.gs.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">REGISTRY_SCALING_APPROVERS_JSON</td><td>Approved governing work-account list; empty/absent disables scaling decisions. Existing source/target grants and independence still apply.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-additions" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="additions"><summary>16. My Work, personal drafts, saved comparisons and profile controls</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>My Work uses the authorized records already available to you to show ownership/role-relevant proposals, execution tasks, review needs and commitments. Open takes you to the appropriate workflow; the view does not widen your permissions.</p><p>Personal drafts are explicitly saved, resumed or deleted for your own account and currently permitted scope. They are not submitted proposals or approval records. The app limits active drafts and payload size, revalidates current fields/options at submission and does not rely on browser local storage or background autosave.</p><p>My History uses real manual aggregate snapshots, bound to the account, exact read-grant context and selected filters. Comparisons are available only where that context matches; there is no invented pre-installation history or automatic claim that a particular action caused a count change.</p><p>The user administration, expiry warnings and handover controls are later approved additions to the sponsor baseline. Read the access section for their boundaries. These additions do not replace the original registration → approval → execution → assessment → verification discipline.</p></div></details>
<details id="sponsorGuide-source-notes" data-registry-guide-section data-sponsor-guide-topic="source-notes"><summary>17. How older screenshots and sponsor notes are reconciled</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content"><p>This guide combines the legible worksheet explanations with the Action Registry parts of the sponsor meetings, BRDs, executive/InfoSec guide and implementation notes. Overlapping explanations are combined rather than counted as separate requirements. The two IMG_4857 photographs are duplicates; cropped edges, unreadable cells and blank examples are not reconstructed.</p><p>The early worksheet’s named L1 and attachment-at-registration wording are superseded by the later confirmed shared independent approval pool and proof-before-completion rule. Early brainstorm categories and the original three-tab/Form/email template are superseded by the supplied five-category/thirteen-type framework, current web-app workspaces and shared Chat. The historic claim that read paths lacked authorization describes an old weakness; the current app checks protected reads server-side using the approved scope policy.</p><p>The older “absolute difference” phrase for Performance Change is reconciled to the explicit Current − Baseline signed formula. Old MYQ/MTQ wording does not authorize renaming historical IDs. Proposed DRAFT-1 percentages remain labeled proposals. Old version labels, historical mock-test counts, planning dates, example savings, delivery screenshots and draft RACI/signature tables are context, not current production approval or proven benefits.</p><p>The broader meetings also discuss WBR automation/basis-point signals, capacity forecasting, Salesforce P0, the TL habit tracker, OMG and Gemini ideas. Those separate initiatives are not features of this registry. A Governance Project may describe work on such an initiative without adding the other application or its integration here.</p><p>Reference basis: worksheet IMG_4854, IMG_4855, IMG_4856 and the duplicate IMG_4857 views; the Action Registry portions of script_2 and script_3; the completed working BRD and recovered DOCX text; the v2.16.6 BRD; the Executive and Infosec Guide; the v2.16.5 Activation and Changes notes; and the original SAMPLE_2 template. Use the current app/Installer for operating steps and the controlled company process for policy/document changes. The older BRD directs document/process requests through ServiceNow to the team’s Documentation Administrator; actual assignments, company approvals and private destination links must be confirmed through approved internal channels.</p></div></details>
</section>

<details id="registryFieldByFieldGuide" data-registry-guide-section>
  <summary>How to fill in an action plan — field-by-field guide</summary>
  <div class="sponsor-guide-content">
    <p>Use this guide when registering a proposal and recording the work that follows. The examples are illustrative, not approved targets or company policy. Choose your actual configured values, authorized people and approved sources. Do not include customer or merchant personal data, account or transaction details, secrets, or raw case records.</p>
    <p><strong>Start:</strong> Open <strong>Submission &amp; Updates</strong> and select <strong>Register New Action Plan</strong>. You need active submission permission and access to the selected scope. You can save an incomplete draft; saving a draft does not submit it for approval. Select <strong>Register for Approval</strong> when the required fields are ready.</p>
    <h4>1. Scope</h4>
    <div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">What to enter</th><th scope="col">Illustrative example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Authorized scope — Site / Department</td><td>Required. Select the configured scope where the action belongs and which you are allowed to use. The action owner must also have access to it. Global is a literal configured scope; selecting it does not automatically grant access elsewhere.</td><td>An authorized configured site such as ARN, if available to you.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Additional Department</td><td>Optional. Select a configured department if it helps describe the work. This is a reporting dimension and does not create cross-site access.</td><td>Quality, if configured.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Channel</td><td>Required. Select the configured operating channel affected by the action.</td><td>Chat, if configured.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>LOB</td><td>Required. Select the configured line of business affected by the action.</td><td>MS Core, if configured.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cohort</td><td>Required. Select the configured population or group covered.</td><td>0–3 Months, if configured.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cohort Details</td><td>Required when the selected cohort is Targeted. Describe the group at an appropriate aggregate level; avoid personal data.</td><td>New-hire group in the selected site and channel.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>2. Action</h4>
    <div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">What to enter</th><th scope="col">Illustrative example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Action Plan Name</td><td>Required. Use a short, specific title that distinguishes this plan.</td><td>Improve coaching consistency.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Action Steps</td><td>Required. Explain who will do what, for which group, when and how execution will be checked. Keep the description within the permitted content rules.</td><td>Team leaders conduct weekly coaching for the selected cohort and record an aggregate completion summary.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Action Owner</td><td>Required. Enter an active, listed <code>@taskus.com</code> work account that can read the selected scope. Ownership assigns responsibility; it does not itself grant approval rights.</td><td>The actual accountable colleague’s listed work email.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Action Category</td><td>Required. Choose the configured strategic category for the work.</td><td>Intervention, if it fits the action.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Action Type</td><td>Required. Select a type allowed under the chosen category; the form shows the relevant guidance.</td><td>Behavior Realignment under Intervention, if appropriate.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Activity reference example</td><td>Optional. Select a suggested activity or leave it unselected and write your own method below.</td><td>Targeted coaching, if relevant.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Activity — execution method</td><td>Required. Describe the method in your own words; one plan remains one action even if the method has several steps.</td><td>Coach against the selected behavior and review aggregate completion weekly.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>3. Measurement and glide path</h4>
    <div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">What to enter</th><th scope="col">Illustrative example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Measurement method</td><td>Numeric KPI is the normal path. The alternative Governance outcome is available only for Standardization / Governance Projects when the result is measured against verifiable criteria rather than a numeric KPI.</td><td>Numeric KPI for a configured Quality measure; Governance outcome for an eligible governance project.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Target KPI / Behavior</td><td>Required for a numeric plan. Choose a configured KPI with an approved unit and direction. Do not infer the unit or direction from its name.</td><td>Quality, only if your Settings defines it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Baseline Performance</td><td>Required for a numeric plan. Enter the starting value in the configured unit. For a percentage, use a value from 0 to 1 or add the percent sign; a Count must be a non-negative whole number. AHT accepts a non-negative number or HH:MM:SS.</td><td>If the approved unit is %, <code>82%</code> or <code>0.82</code>; if AHT, <code>00:05:30</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>End Goal / Target</td><td>Required for a numeric plan. Enter the intended end value using the same unit and input rules as the baseline. If goal equals baseline, percentage progress to goal is not applicable.</td><td>If the approved unit is %, an illustrative target of <code>90%</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Current governance problem</td><td>Required for Governance outcome. State the existing problem or control gap.</td><td>The team lacks a consistently followed handoff process.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Expected governance outcome</td><td>Required for Governance outcome. Describe the intended observable result.</td><td>A documented process is adopted by the selected team.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Acceptance criteria</td><td>Required for Governance outcome. State the conditions that must be met, so they can be checked independently.</td><td>The process is approved, published and acknowledged by the relevant team.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Verification method</td><td>Required for Governance outcome. Explain how and by whom the criteria can be checked. This path does not use numeric KPI, baseline or target fields.</td><td>An authorized reviewer checks the approval record and aggregate acknowledgement.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Time-phased glide path available?</td><td>Required. Select Yes or No. A glide path describes planned KPI or milestone targets over time.</td><td>Yes, if a permitted source already exists; otherwise No.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Glide Path Link</td><td>Required only when Yes. Supply a supported HTTPS Google Drive file or Docs, Sheets or Slides link. When No, leave it blank. Confirm approvers can open the source; saving the link does not change sharing or upload evidence.</td><td>A reviewer-accessible link to the approved planning sheet.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>4. Dates, submission and review</h4>
    <div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">What to enter</th><th scope="col">Illustrative example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Action Status</td><td>A new proposal starts <strong>Not Started</strong>. Approval moves it to Ongoing; do not use this field to claim execution before approval.</td><td>Not Started.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Start Date</td><td>Required. Select the planned start date for the action.</td><td>The agreed start date.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Target End Date</td><td>Required. Select the planned target end date; it cannot be earlier than the start date.</td><td>The agreed completion target.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What changed in this revision?</td><td>Shown when revising a proposal. Explain the actual change and how review feedback was addressed before resubmitting the new version.</td><td>Clarified the population and updated the verification steps.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>After registration the app creates an action ID and records the proposal as Pending Review / Not Started. A separate eligible scoped approver reviews the current version. Returned for Revision and Declined require an explanatory review comment. Approval, execution, leadership comments and effectiveness are distinct records; an authorized admin exception is explicit and audited where permitted.</p>
    <h4>5. After approval: updates, evidence and comments</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Execution update:</strong> The current action owner selects the approved plan and enters the current performance for a numeric plan, the applicable execution status and any useful execution comment. A governance outcome uses the progress comment instead of a numeric current value. Record actual progress; completion alone does not prove impact.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> The owner supplies a non-empty file and evidence description. The application accepts PDF, PNG, JPEG, TXT, CSV, DOCX, XLSX or PPTX with matching file type, up to 10 MB. New completion requires existing proof for the current plan version; completed execution is read-only. Uploading requires the configured evidence service and appropriate access.</li>
      <li><strong>Leadership comment:</strong> Enter the feedback in the separate comment field. It is a dated comment record, not a formal approval, execution update or effectiveness rating. Comment permission and scope are checked.</li>
    </ul>
    <h4>6. Effectiveness assessment and follow-up</h4>
    <p>An eligible scoped assessor reviews an approved Ongoing, On Hold or Completed action. Record the effectiveness status; observation start and end; expected impact, behavior changes and observed impact; expectations result; sustained result; relationship to the action; other contributing factors; and assessment rationale. Observation dates cannot be in the future and must be in order. State what the evidence actually shows, including uncertainty.</p>
    <p>Select current-version evidence files or up to five supported, reviewer-accessible Google source links. Every rating except Insufficient Evidence needs at least one source. Validated requires expectations Met and a Supported relationship. No Observable Effect requires Completed execution and expectations Not Met. For a Governance outcome, explain the evidence against each acceptance criterion.</p>
    <p>Choose Continue, Adjust, Stop or Recommend Scaling; enter the decision rationale, an active scoped decision owner, the next review date, a specific follow-up deliverable and deadline, and an independent active scoped verifier with audit permission. The next review and follow-up deadlines cannot be in the past. A scaling recommendation additionally requires Validated and Sustained results, repeatability and transferability evidence, an Acceptable risk conclusion and rationale. The separately authorized governing decision is recorded later; a recommendation alone does not authorize rollout.</p>
    <p>Use the follow-up record to report progress, request an extension or submit completion evidence as allowed. The independent verifier reviews the submitted evidence before closure. Adjusting, stopping or recommending scaling does not automatically revise the proposal or change the execution status.</p>
  </div>
</details>

<section id="registryInstallHandoff" aria-labelledby="registryInstallHandoffTitle">
<h3 id="registryInstallHandoffTitle">Installation and release handoff</h3>
<details id="registryInstallHandoffSteps" data-registry-guide-section><summary>From a new project or an existing deployment to verified release</summary><div class="sponsor-guide-content">
<p>This checklist supports the four supplied installation files. Before creating or updating a web deployment version, remove Installer.gs and any temporary setup launchers from the project: their public launcher functions are callable through HTML Service while present. Keep an offline copy for controlled maintenance. The installer prepares and migrates the workbook; an authorized operator still supplies approved configuration, verifies the actual Google environment and controls deployment. A completed local test, preview or installer run is not production acceptance.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Choose the correct target and preserve recovery.</strong> For an upgrade, open the existing application project and preserve its workbook, deployment ID and URL. Back up source/version/deployment records, the entire workbook including formulas and history, evidence and private Script Properties in approved storage. Stop all older writing deployments, triggers, forms, integrations and other projects that use this workbook. A maintenance flag does not stop older code. Arrange exclusive workbook editing during migration: direct spreadsheet editors do not share the script lock and can race formula checks and writes. Use an approved change record and maintenance window. Test in an isolated staging project/workbook; a <code>/dev</code> page uses saved code and the same project properties, so it must not be pointed at production for write tests.</li>
<li><strong>For a genuinely new app only:</strong> create an approved empty company workbook and an Apps Script project with the authorized work account. A standalone project can be created at script.google.com; a workbook can open its bound project through Extensions → Apps Script. Add the supplied Code.gs, Installer.gs and HTML file named Index. In Project Settings, show the manifest and copy appsscript.json exactly. It specifies V8, Asia/Manila, Advanced Drive v3, deployer execution, domain-only access and the narrow scopes. Verify the advanced service; if the script uses a standard associated Cloud project, its administrator must also enable the Drive API there. Do not broaden the read policy, domain audience or Drive scope to make setup pass.</li>
<li><strong>Replace source without mixing versions.</strong> In an existing project, replace the matching four files together after backup. Code.gs already includes the old Schema, OperationLedger, AccessControl and Convenience modules; remove their old separate copies and old standalone setup/editor helper copies to avoid duplicate declarations. Keep the HTML name Index. Do not publish a partial replacement. Saving source alone does not update an existing versioned deployment.</li>
<li><strong>Configure the named maintenance account.</strong> In Project Settings → Script Properties, enter the exact workbook ID twice as REGISTRY_SPREADSHEET_ID and REGISTRY_MAINTENANCE_TARGET_CONFIRMED. Set REGISTRY_MAINTENANCE_OWNER and REGISTRY_OPERATION_MAINTAINER_EMAIL to the approved exact work email; retain REGISTRY_READ_POLICY=APPROVAL_SITES. Run as that same active and effective editor account. On UPGRADE it must already have one actual Active and permanent Can_Admin entry, valid unexpired access and no unresolved hold. Follow the opening Installer comments for the window, stopped-writer, backup and change-reference properties. These are explicit attestations, not actions performed by a flag.</li>
<li><strong>Preview and apply the schema.</strong> Installer.gs defaults to FRESH for an approved empty workbook. For an existing registry, explicitly change REGISTRY_INSTALLER_MODE to UPGRADE. Change REGISTRY_INSTALLER_STEP to PREVIEW, save and select runRegistryInstaller in the editor function dropdown, then click Run. Review every blocker and proposed change; copy its exact previewHash into REGISTRY_MAINTENANCE_APPROVED_PREVIEW_HASH. Choose APPLY_SCHEMA, save and run again. This leaves REGISTRY_OPERATION_MAINTENANCE=TRUE and REGISTRY_OPERATION_LEDGER_READY=FALSE. If interrupted, preserve partial changes/history, inspect the migration log and run a new preview. Never clear history to force an apply.</li>
<li><strong>Provision only the initial FRESH scope.</strong> ONLY in the new FRESH admin-only workbook after schema setup, the authorized access owner provisions the unique seeded maintenance admin’s approved initial Approval_Sites using exact configured labels separated by commas. Record approval and before/after values in the maintenance change record. Preserve real boolean cells, expiry fields, the seeded Access_Version and every audit row; use no formulas or text TRUE and grant no other capabilities. This restricted initial setup is not a general sheet-edit bypass. Existing security/lifecycle changes require another authorized app admin or governed recovery. Blank scope deliberately blocks the next retry preview; the app prohibits self-security changes.</li>
<li><strong>Migrate retry records before cutover.</strong> Choose PREVIEW_RETRY and review approvalHash and nextBatchCount; copy the exact hash to REGISTRY_OPERATION_MIGRATION_APPROVAL. Choose APPLY_RETRY. Repeat preview and apply for each remaining batch, up to 200 records per apply. Accepted old keys stay represented in the durable ledger; verified completed copies can be removed from old properties. Uncertain or malformed requests remain held for governed review. A held request must not be deleted or resubmitted with a new key to obtain another save. Choose STATUS to inspect schema, remaining legacy records and configuration blockers.</li>
<li><strong>Finish controlled measurement configuration.</strong> On FRESH, the four source-defined arithmetic formulas are selected by default when REGISTRY_INSTALL_SPONSOR_MATH is absent and no custom formulas are set. Set FALSE or an explicit blank to leave the preset unselected, or TRUE to select it explicitly. PREVIEW shows the current timezone and any proposed change. For a blank FRESH workbook, the approved APPLY sets Asia/Manila before installing formulas; UPGRADE preserves the existing timezone. Alternatively use separately reviewed complete REGISTRY_APPROVED_ARRAY_FORMULAS_JSON with the preset absent, FALSE or blank; never select both. Existing formulas and occupied spill ranges are preserved. The preset supplies no guessed Schedule Health threshold, KPI unit/direction or sponsor-approved stability numbers. Complete those approved settings and test actual formula results. A schema success or readyForStaging flag does not approve business rules.</li>
<li><strong>Provision evidence without expanding permissions.</strong> Keep an existing evidence folder ID and sharing unchanged. For a new destination only, after schema setup explicitly run runRegistryEvidenceFolderCreate from the editor as the exact permanent maintenance admin while all maintenance gates remain satisfied. This optional helper creates one new folder using drive.file, verifies its ID and records EVIDENCE_FOLDER_ID. It reads no business history, changes no sharing and leaves REGISTRY_EVIDENCE_READY=FALSE. Tenant ownership/default sharing remain unverified. It refuses an existing folder or a previous provisioning attempt. A bounded REGISTRY_EVIDENCE_PROVISION_STATE marker is confirmed before creation; an unknown outcome blocks blind retries. Preserve the marker and unique EVP folder name/known ID, independently establish the actual result, then use approved maintenance recovery. Never clear an uncertain marker merely to create another folder. The separate runRegistryEvidenceFolderPreview checks only configured folder metadata and ability to add children. Actual folder ACLs, old artifacts, deployment access and upload/cleanup still need verification before an operator separately enables evidence readiness. An unavailable existing folder is a migration/access issue, not permission to replace it.</li>
<li><strong>Run staging checks with explicit, separate gates.</strong> Only after reviewed migration and approved staging configuration, the authorized operator may set REGISTRY_OPERATION_LEDGER_READY=TRUE and REGISTRY_OPERATION_MAINTENANCE=FALSE on the isolated staging target, retaining REGISTRY_OPERATION_LEGACY_WRITERS_RETIRED=TRUE. Keep REGISTRY_CHAT_MODE=PAUSED and evidence readiness FALSE until each integration is separately verified. Test authorized and denied identities/scopes, versions, upload/cleanup, duplicate/stale saves and uncertainty recovery. Pause again before more maintenance. Configure the real Chat destination privately, approve its audience, preview content and explicitly install its schedules only after authorization. Empty REGISTRY_SCALING_APPROVERS_JSON keeps governing decisions disabled; named accounts still need existing source/target grants, approval capability and independence.</li>
<li><strong>Release only after recorded authorization.</strong> Complete the same reviewed migration/configuration on the confirmed production target with old writers stopped. After acceptance and production authorization, the operator explicitly uses ledger ready TRUE and operation maintenance FALSE; keep legacy writers retired. Before creating or updating the web deployment, remove the entire Installer.gs and any temporary public setup launchers from the project, save, and review the remaining public functions. Keep a protected offline copy for authorized maintenance. For an existing app, Deploy → Manage deployments → Edit and select the accepted new version, then deploy to preserve the existing URL. For a new app, Deploy → New deployment → Web app, retain deployer execution and domain-only access, then verify the actual <code>/exec</code> audience and signed-in identities. Verify real role denials, integrations, triggers and recovery after cutover. Re-pause on failure or uncertainty. Do not restart an old writer against the migrated live ledger.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Official setup references:</strong> Google’s <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/projects" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">project setup</a>, <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/concepts/manifests" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">manifest</a>, <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/services/advanced" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">advanced services</a>, <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/properties" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">properties</a>, <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/concepts/deployments" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">versioned deployments</a>, <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/web" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">web apps</a>, <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/advanced/drive" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Advanced Drive</a> and <a href="https://developers.google.com/workspace/drive/api/guides/api-specific-auth" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Drive scope guidance</a> were checked on 30 October 2026. These explain platform mechanics; they do not verify this tenant or authorize production.</p>
</div></details>
</section>

<details id="registryExistingGuide" data-registry-guide-section><summary>App controls, detailed how-to and glossary</summary>
<div class="card readme-card">
  <h3>Registry Workflow</h3>
  <p class="readme-intro">Registration, approval, execution, effectiveness assessment, and follow-up decisions are separate stages. Use the appropriate workspace for each action.</p>
  <div class="readme-table-wrap"><table class="readme-table"><thead><tr><th>Workspace / control</th><th>Purpose and behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Executive Dashboard</td><td>Portfolio overview, site snapshot, top exceptions, action table, and narrative. Filters also scope the approval queue and Gantt. Screenshot Mode provides an Executive brief and a Full Report with all filtered action rows.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Submission &amp; Updates</td><td>Register a new action, inspect an existing plan, update execution, upload evidence, and view update history.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Approvals &amp; Feedback</td><td>Review pending proposals, approve/return/decline the current plan version, inspect review history, and add leadership feedback. The badge counts pending plans within the current filters, not just plans you can approve.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Register New Action Plan</td><td>Explicitly starts a blank registration. After successful registration or existing-plan selection, the registration fields stay greyed out. Registration requires a verified, active user with Can_Submit checked.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Approve Plan</td><td>Sets the reviewed plan to Ongoing automatically and allows execution. The reviewer must be active, have Can_Approve checked, and have the plan's site or department listed in Approval_Sites. Scopes come from Settings (Site or Site/Department). Global is a literal scope, not permission for every site or department. Approval does not prove execution or effectiveness.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Review independence</td><td>Regular approvers cannot decide a plan they own or submitted for the current plan version. The server checks the current owner and a matching submission audit snapshot. Missing, duplicated, or inconsistent submission provenance blocks new decisions until the maintainer reconciles it; the app does not guess historical submitters.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Admin self-approval exception</td><td>An active admin still needs Can_Approve, explicit read access, and the matching approval scope. For a plan they own or submitted, they must explicitly select the exception and give a meaningful reason. The review records the exception, reason, conflict, reviewer, plan version, and submission provenance. The exception cannot bypass missing provenance, completed-plan locks, or stale-version checks.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Effectiveness &amp; Decisions</td><td>Assess Ongoing, On Hold, or Completed actions using an active account with Can_Audit checked. Approval-enrolled plans need current-version approval. Record impact, behavior changes, observation dates, attribution, evidence, effectiveness rating, and a follow-up decision with an active owner and review date. Each save appends Assessment_Log and updates the master assessment pointer; earlier assessments remain available. Recorded KPI snapshots are latest values at assessment time, not period-specific aggregates.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Assessment defaults</td><td>Owner and expected KPI objective reuse the current plan. The first observation start is suggested from the planned start, explicitly labeled for confirmation; subsequent assessments suggest the day after the previous current-plan observation end. End defaults to today in Manila. Dates remain editable to match evidence, including overlapping periods; future observation dates are blocked. Approval and upload dates are not actual execution or observation dates. Ratings, observed impact and causal explanations remain human judgments.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Google Chat delivery</td><td>Configure the private webhook before enabling delivery. Active Can_Admin maintainers can pause/resume and send assignment notices or eligible due reminders. Notices are consolidated in the shared Chat space, naming owners and escalation contacts. No direct messages or emails are sent. Manual sending does not install the daily trigger; its maintainer installs it in Apps Script.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Verified follow-through</td><td>Each new assessment creates an Open delivery commitment and a separate next effectiveness review commitment. Continue with identical completion and review dates uses one combined review commitment. Each commitment records a deliverable, active owner, independent Can_Audit verifier, completion deadline and a separate effectiveness review date. Owner records progress or blockage, then submits evidence. The verifier closes, returns, or explicitly withdraws it with a reason. Completing a commitment does not prove effectiveness or approve scaling. Earlier plan commitments remain open until explicitly resolved.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Overdue and three strikes</td><td>Deadline dates run through end of day in Asia/Manila. Unresolved commitments are overdue the next day. A confirmed notification suppresses further notices for that commitment and owner/verifier phase for 24 hours. Confirmed overdue messages earn strikes 1, 2 and 3; second and third reminders name mapped manager and executive contacts in the same Chat space. Strikes cap at three. While awaiting verification, the verifier has a separate deadline (default two calendar days) and escalation count. Notes do not reset strikes; approved extensions preserve the original deadline and history. Weekends are included.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Plan change notices</td><td>Successful registrations, revisions, reviews, execution updates, feedback, assessments and follow-up changes append a notice to Registry_Chat_Outbox. Only new changes are queued; historical records are not replayed. Maintainers may preview or send Plan Updates. The separate 15-minute Apps Script trigger delivers queued notices and new assignment messages; the daily trigger handles due reminders. The webhook stays in Script Properties. Pause applies to all Chat delivery. Notification failures do not reverse saved changes. Each queued event is deduplicated; unknown send outcomes require reconciliation before retry.</td></tr><tr><td class="readme-term">Chat operation</td><td>Content previews, routing diagnostics and sends use the current server-authorized records. Active scoped Can_Audit assessors or Can_Admin maintainers may preview; scoped Can_Admin maintainers manage sends, triggers and uncertain outcomes. A site grant never widens business-data reads. Pause/resume changes the shared delivery mode for the registry project. The recorded trigger owner is reauthorized with its current scope on each run. Approved shared-space audience and verified configuration remain prerequisites for actual activation. Notifications are logged before sending; confirmed Chat acceptance earns strikes. Unknown transport or server outcomes remain SENDING until reconciled, preventing blind retries. An accepted message is not proof of reading. Content is summarized to fit bounded shared-space messages; up to 100 eligible commitments are processed per run.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Weekly effectiveness review</td><td>Operations reviews every eligible current-version action each week against evidence and observed impact. The weekly queue uses the app's existing Sunday–Saturday Manila reporting window and the recorded assessment timestamp, separately from saved next-review dates and observation-period dates. It shows reviews needed, reviews recorded this week, missing or inconsistent history, and actions needing evidence or adjustment attention. Only an authorized assessor records a rating. Missing reviews never manufacture an effectiveness rating or change execution. Completed execution stays locked; use the permitted assessment, follow-up or follow-on-plan workflow.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Executive impact reporting</td><td>Effectiveness counts and the Impact narrative follow all dashboard filters. All plans in scope are counted, including Completed and Cancelled. Assessed includes Insufficient Evidence; missing ratings are Not Yet Assessed. Unknown ratings are shown separately and excluded from assessed totals. Completion and goal attainment do not automatically validate impact.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Review accountability</td><td>Current-version review status is grouped by site and department. Eligible reviewers have Active, Can_Approve and matching Approval_Sites; this does not assign a single accountable reviewer. Days waiting use the current plan-version submission date in Manila calendar days, not original creation for revised plans. Missing dates are not estimated. Reviewer activity counts the latest recorded decision per current plan, not a performance score or SLA.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Effectiveness ratings</td><td>Validated: clear evidence of desired impact, expectations Met, and a Supported relationship. Partially Effective: some impact, but insufficient or inconsistent. No Observable Effect: Completed action with expectations Not Met and supporting evidence. Insufficient Evidence: too early or too little evidence to assess impact. File presence alone does not select a rating.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Next decision</td><td>Continue, Adjust, Stop, or Recommend Scaling. Every decision records rationale, active accountable owner, and a next effectiveness review date plus a specific completion commitment. Stop does not automatically cancel execution; Adjust does not revise the approved plan. Use the existing owner workflows when a plan or execution change is needed.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Recommend Scaling</td><td>Requires Validated impact, Sustained results, repeatability evidence, transferability/adaptations, and an Acceptable risk review with explanation. The Scaling Authorization panel records a separate governing Approved or Declined decision, exact target scopes, conditions, rationale and server-dated actor history. Only explicitly configured, independent governing approvers with the necessary source and target permissions can decide. With no approved governing configuration, the form remains disabled. Recording authorization does not create target actions or carry out rollout. The Scaling category alone does not grant approval to scale.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Return / Decline</td><td>A reason is required. A returned proposal must be revised and resubmitted by its owner. Declining a proposal cancels it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Update Execution</td><td>For the permitted action owner: records current performance, execution status, and an update comment. Submit Update retains approval and does not create a new plan version.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Revise Plan</td><td>Changes the proposal and creates a new plan version requiring approval. It resets execution to Not Started and preserves historical reviews and evidence. Cancel Editing exits the revision form.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Leadership Feedback</td><td>Requires Can_Comment. Feedback is independent of approval, execution, and effectiveness; posting a comment does not approve a plan.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Execution Evidence</td><td>Only the active owner may upload when execution is permitted and uploads are configured. Completion requires a current-plan-version file that is still available in the configured folder. A file's presence does not establish adequacy or effectiveness. Maximum 10 MB per file.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Time-phased Glide Path</td><td>Choose Yes or No. Yes requires an HTTPS Google Drive, Docs, Sheets, or Slides link containing dated milestones or KPI targets. Ensure approvers have source access. The app records availability and the link; it does not read the source or calculate actual versus glide path. Source edits are external to plan versioning; changes to approved milestones should be submitted as a proposal revision.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="readme-term">Governance outcome</td><td>Available for Standardization / Governance Projects. Requires current problem, expected outcome, acceptance criteria and verification method instead of numeric KPI values. Owners record progress in update comments and upload execution proof. Assessors explain evidence against each criterion using the same effectiveness ratings. Completion is delivery; Validated is demonstrated impact. Revisions preserve earlier criteria and require new approval. Blank historical measurement methods retain Numeric KPI behavior.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Account Stability</td><td>Uses current-version approval verified against Review_Log. Counts Ongoing/On Hold and recently Completed plans; excludes pending proposals, cancelled plans and legacy plans without recorded approval. Engagement is reported separately. Duplicate/missing IDs or unknown eligible categories withhold the state. Locked-In needs configured recent Validated and Sustained evidence coverage. Default rules are Proposed, not sponsor-approved. The score is Standardization/Scaling share × 100, not proof of KPI health. Every dashboard filter applies to counts; category/status filters withhold the framework state and show Filtered Mix Only. A narrowed scope is labeled. Completed and evidence windows use Manila calendar days. Ties show Mixed / Review Needed. Maintainers edit Stability_Rules and reload; changing a rule does not rewrite history.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Engagement</td><td>Active Action_Framework rows are supported, including Incentivized Activity and Non - Incentivized Activity. These support morale and participation; they are complementary to the primary KPI intervention. Evidence and outcome measurement are still required before claiming effectiveness.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Capture views</td><td>Choose Executive for selected-plan totals, a concise portfolio readout, qualified stability and at most three priority attention items. Choose Full Report for every selected authorized plan in numbered pages with repeated scope, freshness and rule context. Previous/Next changes the displayed report page; Print includes all report pages. Owner fields and owner-filter identities are omitted. Action steps and governance outcomes are excerpts. Review free text before sharing. Capture is blocked until required loads succeed; refreshing exits capture. These views are current snapshots, not saved historical comparisons.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Scope</td><td>Channel, LOB, and Cohort use the Settings lists. A Targeted cohort requires Cohort Details. Approval sites are assigned independently in Access_Control.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">KPI inputs</td><td>Percentages: 82% or 0.82. AHT: HH:MM:SS or decimal minutes. Count: non-negative whole numbers. Other numeric KPIs use their configured units and direction.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Existing records</td><td>Historical Action IDs remain unchanged, including MYQ IDs; their site is displayed as MTQ. Existing plans without Approval_Required enabled retain the legacy execution path.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="readme-term">Loading and saving</td><td>A moving bar indicates work in progress; it is not a measured percentage. Buttons show saving state and prevent repeated submissions. If a save remains unconfirmed, check the current record or contact the maintainer before repeating it. Success is shown only after the server confirms the operation.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
</div>

    <div class="card readme-card">

      <div class="section-heading-row executive-heading-row">
        <div>
          <h3>Registry Reference</h3>
          <div class="executive-section-subtitle">Terms and plain-English descriptions used across the tracker</div>
        </div>
        <div class="filtered-count"><span id="registryReleaseIdentityReadme">Release identity unavailable</span> · Plan approval</div>
      </div>

      <div class="readme-intro">
        Use this page as the reference for the Executive Dashboard, Action Plans table, Top Exceptions, and Gantt Timeline.
        Phase 1 adds access checks, category/type classification, free-text activities, independent feedback, and owner-only evidence uploads.
        Plan approval and evidence-based effectiveness assessment are enabled. Account-stability classification and category maturity scoring are available with visible, configurable rules. Governance Projects can use Governance outcome with required acceptance criteria and evidence-based assessment. Blank historical measurement methods retain Numeric KPI behavior.
        Existing legacy categories and descriptions remain unchanged. KPI improvement or completion does not demonstrate effectiveness.
      </div>

      <div class="readme-table-wrap">
        <table class="readme-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Term</th>
              <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>

            <tr class="readme-section-row">
              <td colspan="2">Executive Summary</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Active Actions</td>
              <td>Actions that are not marked Completed or Cancelled.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">On Track</td>
              <td>Open actions currently classified as within the expected schedule and not flagged At Risk or Overdue.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">At Risk</td>
              <td>Open actions currently flagged as needing attention before the target end date.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Overdue</td>
              <td>Open actions whose Target End Date has already passed.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Goal Met</td>
              <td>The Current Performance has reached or passed the End Goal / Target based on whether the KPI should move higher or lower.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr class="readme-section-row">
              <td colspan="2">Current Review-Week Snapshot</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Current Review-Week Snapshot</td>
              <td>Counts current row states with recorded dates in the current Sunday-Saturday Manila review week. These are latest-record snapshots, not transition counts or comparisons with an earlier review.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Completed</td>
              <td>Actions marked Completed whose Completion Date falls within the current review week.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Goal Met</td>
              <td>Actions currently meeting their KPI target that were updated during the current review week.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Updated · At Risk</td>
              <td>Actions currently At Risk whose latest recorded update is within the current review week. No prior health state is inferred.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Overdue · Due This Week</td>
              <td>Open actions whose Target End Date passed during the current review week and are currently Overdue.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Updated · On Track &amp; Improving</td>
              <td>Actions currently On Track and Improving or Goal Met whose latest recorded update is within the current review week. These records are not labeled as recovered because no earlier state is compared.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr class="readme-section-row">
              <td colspan="2">Performance Terms</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Baseline Performance</td>
              <td>The KPI result recorded when the action plan starts. It is the reference point used to evaluate movement.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Current Performance</td>
              <td>The latest KPI result entered for the action plan.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">End Goal / Target</td>
              <td>The KPI result the action plan is intended to reach.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Outcome</td>
              <td>Observed KPI comparison of Current Performance versus Baseline and Goal, not an effectiveness assessment. The possible results are Goal Met, Improving, Unchanged, Worsening, or Insufficient Data.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Improving</td>
              <td>Current Performance has moved from the Baseline in the correct direction toward the Goal but has not reached the Goal yet.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Unchanged</td>
              <td>Current Performance is effectively still at the Baseline.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Worsening</td>
              <td>Current Performance has moved away from the Goal compared with the Baseline.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Insufficient Data</td>
              <td>The tracker does not have enough valid KPI information to determine the Outcome.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr class="readme-section-row">
              <td colspan="2">Action Plan Terms</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Action Status</td>
              <td>The workflow state selected for the action plan, such as Not Started, Ongoing, On Hold, Completed, or Cancelled.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Days Left</td>
              <td>Calendar days remaining until the Target End Date. 0 displays as Due Today. A negative number means the action is past due. Completed or Cancelled actions display —.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td class="readme-term">Action Owner</td>
              <td>The accountable owner: an active, listed work account. Only the verified owner may update execution and upload evidence; submission and comment flags are independent.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr><td class="readme-term">Activity / Action Steps</td><td>Activity is the free-text execution method. Action Steps describe specifically how that method will be performed. Several activities in text still represent one plan and one Action ID.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="readme-term">Leadership Feedback</td><td>Independent, timestamped comments by active users with Can_Comment checked. They do not modify execution, KPI results, review, or effectiveness.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="readme-term">Execution Evidence</td><td>Completed plans have read-only execution, plan details and execution evidence; saved history remains visible and separate effectiveness assessments remain available. Upload a supporting file before completion. Not Started plans may add proof later. New transitions to Completed require an available artifact for the current plan version; historical Completed records are retained. File presence is not validation of impact.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="readme-term">Review / Effectiveness</td><td>Separate from execution and KPI outcome. New plans are Pending Review and Not Yet Assessed. Unrecorded legacy reviews display Not Recorded. Approvers need an active account, Can_Approve checked, and the action site in Approval_Sites. New plans await approval before execution. Active users with Can_Audit may assess approved Ongoing, On Hold, and Completed plans in Effectiveness &amp; Decisions. Assessment ratings do not change execution automatically.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="readme-term">Scope and reporting limits</td><td>Existing site, owner, KPI, category and status filters apply to the snapshot, action rows, and report views. Department filtering, category mix, effectiveness counts and review queues follow the same scope. Category mix describes recorded plans. Account Stability separately evaluates approved active and recently completed core plans using its visible rules and recent evidence coverage. Full Report labels report pages and row ranges; wrapped content may continue onto another physical printed sheet.</td></tr>

            <tr>
